================================================================================
sws - Simple Web Server (CS631 Advanced Programming in the UNIX Environment)
================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW
-----------
sws is a lightweight, secure HTTP/1.0 web server conforming to RFC 1945 and
RFC 3875 (Common Gateway Interface v1.1). The server adheres strictly to the
specifications in the sws(1) manual page and follows the programming practices
and NetBSD Kernel Normal Form (KNF) style guidelines from Stevens CS631 APUE.

sws operates either as a true background UNIX daemon capable of handling
multiple concurrent connections via multi-process worker forking, or in
debugging mode (-d), which retains the process in the foreground, handles
connections sequentially, and prints request logs directly to stdout.


2. ARCHITECTURE AND CODE ORGANIZATION
-------------------------------------
The codebase is structured into modular components:

* sws.c / sws.h
  Main entry point, command-line argument parsing (getopt), daemonization,
  signal handling (SIGCHLD, SIGPIPE, SIGINT, SIGTERM), and the master connection
  accept loop.

* net.c / net.h
  Network socket lifecycle management. Implements dual-stack IPv4/IPv6 socket
  binding when no -i address is provided (binding to :: with IPV6_V6ONLY and
  to 0.0.0.0), connection polling via poll(2), socket option configuration
  (SO_REUSEADDR), and remote peer IP extraction using getnameinfo(3).

* http.c / http.h
  HTTP protocol engine implementing RFC 1945. Handles request line reading with
  timeout bounds, method validation (GET and HEAD supported; other methods yield
  501 Not Implemented), HTTP/1.1 downgrade to HTTP/1.0, HTTP/0.9 Simple-Request
  detection, case-insensitive parsing of the If-Modified-Since header (RFC 1123,
  RFC 850, and asctime formats), and structured response generation.

* file.c / file.h
  Filesystem interaction layer. Implements path traversal mitigation and
  sandboxing, user directory expansion (/~<user>/ -> ~<user>/sws/), directory
  index resolution (serving index.html if present, or dynamically generating
  an alphanumeric HTML directory listing while omitting dotfiles), regular file
  streaming, and dynamic MIME type identification via libmagic (magic(5)).

* cgi.c / cgi.h
  CGI/1.1 execution engine implementing RFC 3875. Matches /cgi-bin requests when
  -c dir is specified, extracts SCRIPT_NAME, PATH_INFO, and QUERY_STRING,
  verifies execution permissions, constructs the RFC 3875 environment variable
  table, creates pipes, forks worker processes, enforces execution timeouts,
  parses CGI headers (including Status:), and streams CGI responses to clients.

* log.c / log.h
  Logging subsystem implementing the Apache Common Log format variation:
  '%a %t "%r" %>s %b'. Handles thread-safe atomic appending to log files (-l)
  or stdout routing in debug mode (-d).

* compat.c / compat.h
  Cross-platform portability shims providing size-bounded string functions
  (strlcpy, strlcat), program identification (getprogname, setprogname),
  and time conversion (timegm) across NetBSD, Linux, and OmniOS.


3. PROTOCOL AND FEATURE IMPLEMENTATION
--------------------------------------
* HTTP Methods and RFC 1945:
  sws speaks a restricted dialect of HTTP/1.0. It accepts GET and HEAD methods.
  Requests with other HTTP methods (POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS, etc.) trigger
  501 Not Implemented. HTTP/1.1 requests are accepted and quietly answered with
  HTTP/1.0 response lines. HTTP/0.9 Simple-Requests (e.g., "GET /") return only
  the entity body without status headers.

* Header Support:
  sws systematically generates Date, Server, Last-Modified, Content-Type, and
  Content-Length headers on Full-Responses. For conditional GET requests,
  sws evaluates the If-Modified-Since request header against the file's
  st_mtime; if the file has not been modified, a 304 Not Modified response is
  returned with no message body.

* Content-Type and magic(5):
  MIME types are determined using libmagic (magic_open(MAGIC_MIME_TYPE),
  magic_load, and magic_file). If libmagic is unable to identify a file, it
  gracefully defaults to "application/octet-stream". Dynamically generated
  directory listings and error responses use "text/html".

* Directory Indexing:
  When a request targets a directory, sws checks for an "index.html" file.
  If found, that file is served. If absent, sws inspects the directory using
  opendir(3) and readdir(3), ignores any entry beginning with a "." (including
  "." and ".."), sorts remaining entries alphanumerically using qsort(3) and
  strcmp(3), and renders a clean HTML directory listing with links.

* User Directories:
  URIs beginning with "/~" trigger user directory translation. The string
  between "/~" and the subsequent "/" is interpreted as a username and queried
  via getpwnam(3). If valid, requests resolve into "<pw_dir>/sws/".

* CGI Execution (RFC 3875):
  If the -c dir option is supplied, requests beginning with "/cgi-bin" resolve
  scripts relative to the specified CGI directory. The server forks a child
  process, sets up the full standard CGI environment (REQUEST_METHOD,
  SCRIPT_NAME, PATH_INFO, PATH_TRANSLATED, QUERY_STRING, REMOTE_ADDR,
  SERVER_SOFTWARE, GATEWAY_INTERFACE, HTTP_*, etc.), attaches stdout to a pipe,
  and executes the script. The parent parses the CGI script output for headers
  and encapsulates the output in a valid HTTP/1.0 response.

* Logging:
  Enabled via -l file or in debug mode (-d). Conforms to:
  '%a %t "%r" %>s %b'
  - %a: Remote IPv4 or IPv6 client address
  - %t: Request arrival timestamp in GMT (e.g., 2019-10-28T12:12:12Z)
  - %r: First line of the request in quotes (e.g., "GET / HTTP/1.0")
  - %>s: HTTP response status code
  - %b: Response body length in bytes


4. CONCURRENCY AND DAEMONIZATION
--------------------------------
* Daemon Mode (Default):
  Unless -d is provided, sws executes a standard Stevens two-step daemonization:
  forking, calling setsid(2), ignoring SIGHUP, forking a second time to ensure
  no controlling terminal can be reacquired, changing directory to "/", clearing
  the umask, and redirecting stdin, stdout, and stderr to /dev/null.
  Incoming connections are accepted concurrently by forking a child worker
  process per client. The parent server reaps terminated children
  asynchronously using a non-blocking waitpid(2) loop within a SIGCHLD handler.

* Debug Mode (-d):
  When invoked with -d, sws remains in the foreground, handles incoming
  connections synchronously one at a time, and outputs all access logs to
  stdout.


5. SECURITY MEASURES
--------------------
* Path Traversal Prevention:
  Path inputs undergo canonicalization and verification. The server sanitizes
  relative segments, eliminates "." and "..", and resolves real paths using
  realpath(3). If the canonicalized path falls outside the designated docroot,
  user sws directory, or CGI directory, the request is rejected with 403
  Forbidden or 404 Not Found.

* Denial of Service & Hanging Script Protection:
  Socket reads on client connections are bounded by poll(2) timeouts
  (READ_TIMEOUT_SEC = 30s) to prevent slow-loris connection exhaustion.
  CGI execution is similarly monitored with poll(2) timeouts; runaway scripts
  are terminated with SIGKILL if they exceed CGI_TIMEOUT_SEC.

* Signal Safety:
  SIGPIPE is ignored to prevent process termination when remote clients close
  TCP connections prematurely during write operations.


6. PORTABILITY AND SUPPORTED PLATFORMS
--------------------------------------
sws has been verified to compile cleanly with strict compiler flags:
  CFLAGS = -Wall -Werror -Wextra -Wformat=2 -Wpedantic -Wshadow
across all three required platforms:
  1. NetBSD 11.0 (standard reference platform)
  2. Linux (linux.cs631.dotwtf.wtf)
  3. OmniOS (omnios.cs631.dotwtf.wtf)

Under OmniOS (illumos/SunOS), the Makefile dynamically detects the OS using
`uname -s` and automatically appends `-lsocket -lnsl` to LDLIBS, ensuring
flawless builds on all targets without manual intervention.


