8. PROBLEMS OVERCOME AND LESSONS LEARNED
----------------------------------------
* Dual-Stack Socket Binding:
  On NetBSD, IPv6 sockets have IPV6_V6ONLY enabled by default, whereas on some
  Linux distributions it defaults to disabled. To ensure consistent behavior
  across NetBSD, Linux, and OmniOS, we explicitly configure IPV6_V6ONLY on the
  IPv6 socket and maintain a separate IPv4 socket. poll(2) monitors both
  listening sockets seamlessly.

* Path Sandboxing and Relative Paths:
  When a program daemonizes and executes chdir("/"), relative paths provided on
  the command line (such as docroot, -c dir, or -l file) can become invalid.
  We solved this by resolving all relative paths to absolute canonical paths
  using realpath(3) and getcwd(3) before calling daemonize().

* Zombie Child Reaping:
  When using fork(2) for concurrency, child processes become zombies if not
  reaped. Installing a SIGCHLD handler with SA_RESTART and looping over
  waitpid(-1, NULL, WNOHANG) guarantees that all terminated workers are reaped
  without blocking or interrupting active system calls.


9. TIME ESTIMATE
----------------
Approximately 45 hours of combined team effort.
